<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Gezer</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 BCE – 950 BCE (approx.) · capital Gezer</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gezer, a Canaanite city on the road from Egypt to Jerusalem, captured by pharaohs and, by biblical tradition, given to Solomon as a dowry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gezer lay on a hill at the edge of the Judaean foothills, overlooking the coastal road. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> it was fortified with massive walls and gates, and a row of tall standing stones marked a cultic high place that still stands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> local kings ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> lists Gezer among his conquests, its kings Milkilu and Yapahu wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a> boasts that Gezer was seized. The Gezer calendar, an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> inscription, was found there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Gezer kept its independence after the end of Egyptian rule until a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> burned it, according to 1 Kings, and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel</a>. Its later Israelite gate is often linked to that era.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gezer.jpg" alt="Gezer" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: זאב שטיין · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:88338_gazar_mound_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 BCE – 950 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gezer</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Milkilu, Yapahu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Canaanite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Canaanite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Canaanite Gezer</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1375&amp;focus=gezer" aria-label="Gezer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5579 1018 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gezer lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5899 1274L5863 1489"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5863 1489h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1274h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1274h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1375&amp;focus=gezer">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

